# Mandatory author-exclusion requirements — 2026-09-28

## Rule and present status

Exclude `__PRIVATE_VALUE_1__` and `__PRIVATE_VALUE_2__` using exact `lower(trim(username))` matching. `__PRIVATE_VALUE_3__` is the active account. Drop a game if either player matches. Exclude both accounts from account-level analyses, cohorts, donor/instrument populations, normalization/reference samples, and behavioral history construction.

This is an author-requested sample restriction added on September 28, 2026, after prior results had been viewed. Record that timing. It is not a claim of earlier preregistration or a blind robustness decision. Do not assert that existing numerical results already implement it.

## Consume the new main sample

Add this package's `code` directory to the Python import path in the next analysis package. Authenticate the new input manifest rather than bypassing an older producer's checksum checks:

```python
from consume import main_files
from io_tools import scan

paths = main_files('/Volumes/XT_Pro/lichess_kindness')
# Use this source in the analysis's original selection/projection query.
source_sql = scan(paths)
```

This returns the 24 full-width author-excluded main files after validating their policy, months, checksums, and row counts. It neither runs models nor redirects old programs. Use a new output namespace and record the exclusion policy hash, the new main manifest hash, and the final analytical row-set identity in every new result receipt.

The main panel preserves retained row values, including any pre-existing fields. Row exclusion alone does not certify that any inherited sample-dependent aggregate is author-free. Rebuild such fields from filtered events.

## Other sample types

| Consumer | Required implementation |
|---|---|
| Opportunity/game rows with names | Filter both player names before any sample-dependent aggregation, fitting, or eligibility thresholds. |
| Account/profile rows with names | Exclude either author account before joins and account-level fitting. Preserve raw profile snapshots. |
| Numeric-ID-only rows | Use only verified global user-ID mappings; filter every relevant role. Missing mappings must stop the numeric-only consumer. |
| Game-ID-only rows | Anti-join the audited excluded-game index within its recorded input scope. New or other game sources require their own username/ID exclusion. |
| Receipt episodes and receipt histories | Remove games involving either account from receipt-event construction, exposure counts, giver/recipient histories, donor propensities, and instrument/reference populations. Rebuild affected risk sets and aggregates. |
| Persistence, Patron, kindness scores | Remove author rows and recompute sample-dependent account summaries, weights, cutoffs, ranks, standardizations, and estimates. |
| Mechanism and color models | Refresh fits and pre-policy baselines on the filtered sample; propagate changed coefficients into forecasts. Do not relabel old frozen fits as author-excluded. |
| A2 responsibility/trajectory work | Keep finished engine features. Remove matching targets, apply account exclusion, rebuild any affected behavioral histories, and refit on the retained frozen-sample subset. Report attrition; no replacement sampling to top up the roster. |
| Descriptive tables, figures, manuscript | Regenerate counts, denominators, coefficients, uncertainty, and displayed results after the affected analysis refresh. |

Reusable exact filter:

```python
from exclusion import exact_keep_sql

keep = exact_keep_sql(
    name_columns=['chooser_username_norm', 'disconnected_username_norm'],
    alias='s',
)
# SELECT ... FROM source s WHERE <keep>
```

For numeric rows, load `author_id_resolution.author_ids` from the new ready manifest and call `exact_keep_sql(numeric_columns=['chooser', 'opponent'], author_ids=...)`. Both accounts must be mapped, with distinct nonnegative IDs. Never infer an ID from username order, a username hash, or an unrelated ID namespace. Unresolved numeric mappings do not invalidate exact name filtering; they block consumers that have only numeric IDs. The runner resolves IDs from certified core/main observations and, when available, the existing `glicko2_replay/user_dictionary_v1` files.

For compact game-only inputs:

```python
from consume import excluded_games
from io_tools import scan

index = excluded_games(project, family='main_24m', months=['2023-11'])
# SELECT s.* FROM compact_source s
# ANTI JOIN <scan(index)> e USING (game_id)
```

The index contains the union of author games in the audited opportunity panels. Core/main overlaps are deduplicated by game ID, with month consistency checked. It is not an all-game exclusion database. Do not use it alone to certify an unrelated source, a different month, all-game donor events, or account rows without matching opportunity games.

## Preserve factual histories

Keep original raw archives, factual all-game timestamps, rating state/replay inputs, observed source/adjacency evidence, and engine caches. Actual ratings, real previous-game identities, factual starts/activity, account tenure, and factual prior losses describe what happened. Do not simulate a counterfactual chronology with the author removed. Continue to derive adjacency from the true sequence of games.

Separately, analytical kindness/receipt events and aggregates must implement the exclusion. When a feature combines factual all-game history with behavioral events, preserve the factual time axis and remove author-involving events from the behavioral numerator/reference population. A downstream audit must state which treatment each feature uses.

## Required checks before reporting refreshed results

1. Confirm zero author accounts in all account samples/reference populations and zero author-involving games in all analytical event samples.
2. Report removed opportunities and grants by month and role, account attrition, and pre/post row-set identities. Core and main are overlapping populations; do not add their counts together.
3. Recompute affected histories, donor/reference scores, thresholds, model fits, weights, uncertainty, forecasts, and paper outputs. A direct-game hit count of zero alone does not clear an analysis that depends on another affected population.
4. Compare the refreshed estimates with the preserved prior results. Label both sample definitions and the date of this decision.
5. Only after execution and review may the manuscript state: “Games involving either of the author's two accounts are excluded from all analytical samples.” Keep account names in the private replication policy unless their publication is separately intended.

The current package completes the filtering/audit infrastructure. It leaves statistical re-estimation and paper updates explicitly pending. No unseen account count or removal count is assumed.
